course: dictionary
lesson: 5412
title: A labourer's work
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A labourer is a person whose job is hard physical work, such as building, digging or farming. The clue is in the work: it needs strength and effort, not special qualifications. «The Suez Canal was dug largely by Egyptian workers, tens of thousands of them forced labourers.» Digging a canal by hand is hard physical work, so these workers were labourers.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «A builder's labourer carries bricks on a building site.» If you put "a worker who does heavy jobs on the site" in place of labourer, the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Some labourers were forced, which means they were made to work and could not choose. «Peter the Great built St Petersburg on the Baltic coast from 1703» and many forced labourers died building it on marshy land. History books tell us this sadly, so the word can carry serious meaning in history.
explain: Labourers worked in many places, from farms to railways to factories. In the Bible, Jesus told a parable about labourers in a vineyard, which shows the word has been used for a very long time. Spelling note: in American English it is spelled laborer.
fact: A labourer is a person whose job is hard physical work, such as building, digging or farming.
fact: Labourers usually work without special training.
fact: A builder's labourer carries bricks on a building site.
fact: Labourers built canals and railways, and worked on farms and in factories.
fact: The American spelling is laborer.
exercise: What is a labourer? | *A person whose job is hard physical work, such as building or digging | A person who paints pictures for a gallery | A person who teaches children in a school | A person who sells food in a shop
exercise: Which job would a labourer most likely do? | *Carrying bricks on a building site | Selling tickets at a cinema | Serving tea in a café | Writing letters in an office
exercise: Does a labourer usually need special training? | Yes, but only if they work in a bank | *Usually not, because the job is mostly hard physical work | Yes, always, for many years at university | Only if they work on a farm
test: In «The Suez Canal was dug largely by Egyptian workers, tens of thousands of them forced labourers», what kind of work were the labourers doing? | *Digging | Singing in a choir | Painting pictures | Cooking meals
test: Which of these people is a labourer? | A driver selling tickets | A doctor checking a patient | A teacher reading a story | *A farm worker digging a field
exam: Which phrase names a labourer who works on a building site? | music labourer | *builder's labourer | sea labourer | paper labourer
